WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.896
Archive handling deserves special review because an archive is a
collection of file-related instructions.

2
00:00:05.896 --> 00:00:12.496
Entries may include names, directories, metadata, permissions, or link-
like behavior depending on the format and tool.

3
00:00:12.496 --> 00:00:25.377
Safe extraction should validate each entry, choose a trusted extraction
destination, enforce containment, avoid unexpected overwrite behavior, and
treat extracted content as untrusted until it has passed the rest of the
application?

4
00:00:25.377 --> 00:00:26.402
s policy.

5
00:00:26.402 --> 00:00:32.426
Symbolic links, shortcuts, hard links, and mounted paths can affect where
a file operation ultimately points.

6
00:00:32.426 --> 00:00:40.821
The details vary by platform and deployment, but the design concern is
stable: a path that looked safe during validation may refer somewhere else
when used.

7
00:00:40.821 --> 00:00:52.933
Teams should understand whether their APIs follow links, whether storage
directories can contain links, and whether untrusted users can create or
influence link-like objects inside locations the application processes.

8
00:00:52.933 --> 00:01:00.623
Temporary files are security-relevant because they often handle uploads,
generated reports, exports, conversions, or intermediate data.

9
00:01:00.623 --> 00:01:07.416
Predictable names, shared directories, broad permissions, incomplete
cleanup, and accidental reuse can create exposure.

10
00:01:07.416 --> 00:01:18.823
Safer patterns use framework or platform helpers that create unpredictable
names with appropriate permissions, keep temporary data in approved
locations, and clean up consistently after success or failure.

11
00:01:18.823 --> 00:01:23.565
Time-of-check and time-of-use concerns appear when validation and use are
separated.

12
00:01:23.565 --> 00:01:30.614
A file may be replaced, moved, linked, deleted, or modified between the
moment code checks it and the moment code opens or serves it.

13
00:01:30.614 --> 00:01:33.113
Concurrent workers can make this more likely.

14
00:01:33.113 --> 00:01:41.957
Sensitive file workflows should minimize gaps, control permissions, avoid
trusting mutable paths, and prefer stable storage records or handles where
possible.

15
00:01:41.957 --> 00:01:44.520
Sequencing matters as much as the check itself.
